---
import Page from './Page.astro';
import {localPath as href} from '../paths';
const page={title:'Krigsvold for Astro',eyebrow:'The Astro reference',summary:'Astro components and examples for publishing, services, and applications.',heroVariant:'signature'};
---
<Page page={page} current="/"><section class="section"><div class="container jekyll-document"><article class="content editorial-content">
<h2>A smaller engine. The same language.</h2>
<p>This is a working Astro template built from the accepted <strong>Krigsvold reference shell</strong>. Its stylesheet and approved photography are canonical exports. Astro supplies native components and static pages; the browser receives ordinary HTML.</p>
<h3>Three compositions to inspect</h3><ul><li><a href={href('/language/')}>The language</a> uses a document with a focused reading measure.</li><li><a href={href('/journal/what-a-shell-should-own/')}>A journal entry</a> uses a narrow reading column, date, author, and tags.</li><li><a href={href('/setup/')}>Use this template</a> explains how to build it and replace the content.</li></ul>
<h2>A shell with a job</h2><p>The masthead holds the title and context. Navigation leads to destinations. Authors can add manual inline contents above a document. The footer closes the page. Those relationships survive a different rendering engine.</p>
<details><summary>A note from the workshop</summary><p>Polar bears have declined to review the spacing scale.</p></details>
<h2>The complete example library</h2><p><a href={href('/examples/')}>Explore all 50 page recipes</a>: publishing, projects, collections, people, documentation, commerce, events, reports, support, recovery, and application interfaces. The same fictional institute and content appear in both themes.</p><p><a href={href('/examples/integrations/')}>Read about integration boundaries</a> before connecting forms, accounts, devices, or transactions to a live service.</p>
</article></div></section></Page>
